WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:02.520
MRS.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:03.520
CLINTON: Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:04.520
(Applause.)

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:05.520
Well, thank you.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.690
Thank you very much.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:37.791
I have the great pleasure to introduce our
next two speakers, who are about to have a

00:00:37.791 --> 00:00:41.910
conversation concerning health care.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:47.370
And I thought hard about how to introduce
these two men.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:48.660
(Laughter.)

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:54.590
And the more I thought about it, the more
I realized how much they have in common.

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.489
They are both left-handed.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:57.489
(Laughter.)

00:00:57.489 --> 00:01:04.780
They both love golf, a game that does not
often reciprocate the love they put into it.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:05.780
(Laughter.)

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:15.709
They both are fanatic sports fans and go to
great lengths to be in front of the TV or

00:01:15.709 --> 00:01:18.639
on the side of the court or the field.

00:01:18.639 --> 00:01:20.490
They both are master politicians.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:25.209
Each of them has only lost one election.

00:01:25.209 --> 00:01:27.429
(Laughter.)

00:01:27.429 --> 00:01:30.779
They are both Democrats.

00:01:30.779 --> 00:01:33.920
They have fabulous daughters.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.330
(Laughter.)

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:45.280
They each married far above themselves.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.140
(Laughter and applause.)

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.169
And they each love our country.

00:01:52.169 --> 00:02:00.960
And so please join me in welcoming Number
42 and Number 44, Bill Clinton and President

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.419
Barack Obama.

00:02:04.419 --> 00:02:07.450
(Applause.)

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:19.560
PRESIDENT OBAMA: Mr. President.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.590
(Laughter.)

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:32.050
PRESIDENT CLINTON: Are you interviewing me?

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:33.050
PRESIDENT OBAMA: That would be bad.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:34.050
I've been talking a lot today.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.810
PRESIDENT CLINTON: I want to thank you for
giving Hillary a job.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:39.060
That was a good thing you did.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.250
(Laughter.)

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.260
Thank you for coming.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:45.390
PRESIDENT OBAMA: Well, it is wonderful to
be back.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:51.540
And let me start just by saying to all the
people who have for years now supported the

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:56.760
incredible efforts of CGI, thank you.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:02.220
Because wherever we travel, all across the
globe, we see the impact that it's making

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.620
every single day.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:07.340
And we're very proud of what you all do.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:15.490
And let me say that we still miss our former
Secretary of State.

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:16.490
(Applause.)

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:26.210
And I should add that there's nothing she
said that was not true, particularly the part

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:27.670
about us marrying up.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:28.670
(Laughter.)

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:32.440
PRESIDENT CLINTON: Well, that brings me to
my first health care comment.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:37.950
This is going to be a conversation about domestic
and international health, and America's role

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:38.950
in it.

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:44.600
But I want to begin by telling you that I
think the First Lady has done a great job

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.330
in this fight against childhood obesity.

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:52.840
We have been honored at our foundation to
be asked to represent her effort in 18,000

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:58.740
schools where we've lowered the calories in
drinks being served in schools by 90 percent.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:01.040
But she has been great on that one.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:09.350
The other thing I think is that I was a little
upset -- and, as you know, called one of your

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:15.401
administration members when you got to Africa
-- when I read an article that said that you

00:04:15.401 --> 00:04:17.040
didn't have a big initiative in Africa.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.779
And I said -- I can't say exactly what I said
-- (laughter) -- but I said that is inaccurate.

00:04:22.779 --> 00:04:23.779
(Laughter.)

00:04:23.779 --> 00:04:25.949
That's the sanitized version of what I said.

00:04:25.949 --> 00:04:26.949
(Laughter.)

00:04:26.949 --> 00:04:32.570
Because when the President took office, our
program, begun under President Bush, PEPFAR,

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:38.680
was giving antiretroviral medicine to 1.7
million people.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:43.550
Because of an agreement that I made with President
Bush to use generic drugs that were approved

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:49.680
by the FDA, about half our drugs were being
purchased in that way.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:52.449
Under President Obama, we've gone to 99 percent.

00:04:52.449 --> 00:04:59.279
We are treating more than 5.1 million, three
times as many for less money.

00:04:59.279 --> 00:05:00.279
(Applause.)

00:05:00.279 --> 00:05:05.189
That is a stunning legacy -- so that more
money has been put into malaria medicine,

00:05:05.189 --> 00:05:12.560
bed nets, so you saved a lot of money and
saved more lives while doing it.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:13.909
And I'm very proud of that.

00:05:13.909 --> 00:05:15.500
And I want to thank you for it.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.979
It's important.

00:05:17.979 --> 00:05:20.460
(Applause.)

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:26.100
Now, maybe at the end of this conversation
we can get back to some of your current global

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.100
health initiatives.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:34.220
But let's talk a little about the health care
law, because we're about to begin on October

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:38.599
1st open enrollment for six months.

00:05:38.599 --> 00:05:48.340
And I'd like to give you a chance, first of
all, to tell them why -- when you took office,

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:51.439
we were teetering on the brink of a depression.

00:05:51.439 --> 00:05:52.490
You had to avert it.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.139
You had to start the recovery again.

00:05:54.139 --> 00:05:59.249
Why in the midst of all this grief did you
also take on this complex issue?

00:05:59.249 --> 00:06:06.199
Many people were saying, why doesn't he just
focus on the economy and leave this alone?

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:09.029
So tell us why you did it.

00:06:09.029 --> 00:06:16.610
PRESIDENT OBAMA: Well, first of all, I think
it's important to remember that health care

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:18.490
is the economy.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:22.610
A massive part of our economy.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:31.050
And so the idea that somehow we can separate
out the two is a fallacy.

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:40.960
Second of all, the effort for us to deal with
a multifaceted health care crisis has been

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.009
going on for decades.

00:06:43.009 --> 00:06:51.430
And the person who just introduced us, as
well as you, early in your presidency, had

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:58.180
as much to do with helping to shape the conversation
as anybody.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:08.529
The fact is that we have been, up until recently,
the only advanced industrialized nation on

00:07:08.529 --> 00:07:20.009
Earth that permits large numbers of its people
to languish without health insurance.

00:07:20.009 --> 00:07:26.330
Not only is there the cruelty of people who
are unable to get health insurance having

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:35.120
to use the emergency room as their doctor
or their health service, but -- we're also

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:42.210
more efficient than anybody else and so when
we talk about, for example, our deficit -- you

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:50.029
know this better than anybody -- the reason
that we have not only current deficits but

00:07:50.029 --> 00:07:57.099
also projected long-term deficits -- the structural
deficit that we have is primarily based on

00:07:57.099 --> 00:08:03.869
the fact that we have a hugely inefficient,
wildly expensive health care system that does

00:08:03.869 --> 00:08:06.199
not produce better outcomes.

00:08:06.199 --> 00:08:12.639
And if we spent the same amount of money on
health care that Canada or France or Great

00:08:12.639 --> 00:08:20.029
Britain did, or Japan, or any other industrialized
country, with the same outcomes or better

00:08:20.029 --> 00:08:26.869
outcomes, that essentially would remove our
structural deficit, which would then free

00:08:26.869 --> 00:08:34.250
up dollars for us to invest in early-childhood
education and infrastructure and medical research

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:39.010
and all the other things that can make sure
that we're competitive and growing rapidly

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:40.760
over the long term.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:45.070
So my view when I came into office was we've
got an immediate crisis -- we've got to get

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:47.780
the economy growing.

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:52.460
But what we also have to do is to start tackling
some of these structural problems that had

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:55.140
been building up for years.

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:58.240
And one of the biggest structural problems
was health care.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:00.040
It's what accounts for our deficit.

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:01.880
It's what accounts for our debt.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:06.300
It causes pain and misery to millions of people
all across the country.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:09.770
It is a huge burden on our businesses.

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:18.010
I was out at a Ford plant out in Missouri,
making the F series out there, and this is

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:20.440
a big stamping plant.

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:25.950
Ford is now the biggest seller in the United
States.

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:31.320
We took that lead back from the Japanese automakers.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:37.400
But we are still burdened by the fact that
every U.S. automobile that is manufactured

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:44.310
requires a couple of thousand dollars in added
health care costs that our foreign competitors

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:46.010
don't have to pay.

00:09:46.010 --> 00:09:54.260
So this has everything to do with the economy,
in addition to what I consider to be the moral

00:09:54.260 --> 00:10:01.190
imperative that a mom should not have to go
bankrupt if her son or daughter gets sick;

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:10.930
that a family who's dealing with a layoff
and is already struggling to pay the bills

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:16.500
shouldn't also be wondering whether they're
one illness away from losing their home.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:19.880
And I think most Americans agree with that.

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:20.880
(Applause.)

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:30.990
PRESIDENT CLINTON: So first of all, folks,
for those of you who are from the United States,

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:36.050
that's about as good an overview as you're
ever going to hear of what this economic issue

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:37.390
is.

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:44.130
But you remember the President said our structural
deficit would disappear if we had a comparable

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:49.690
health care system in terms of cost to the
French and Germans that are consistently rated

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:50.750
the highest.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:56.620
It's about a trillion dollars a year, and
somewhere around 44 percent of that money

00:10:56.620 --> 00:11:01.190
is government-funded money.

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:03.780
So you just run the numbers.

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:08.610
Think -- over half of our deficit has already
disappeared because of economic growth and

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:11.400
the revenues you raised and the spending we
cut.

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:16.840
And you pretty much get rid of the rest of
it if we just had a comparably expensive system

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:18.750
to any other country.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:24.440
Before you took office, we lost a car company
that wanted to locate in Michigan that went

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:31.950
instead to Canada, and they announced -- they
said, look, we're a car company that provides

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:36.420
health care benefits to an employee, were
not a health care company that sells cars

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:37.800
to cover our bills.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:41.640
We have to go to Canada.

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:46.160
And it was one of the few companies willing
to go on record and say this.

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:48.990
So thank you for doing it.

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:52.180
So let's talk about this.

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:54.740
What does this open enrollment mean?

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:58.250
How are people going to get involved?

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:02.560
When you have universal enrollment you can
manage your costs better and cut inflation

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:03.560
down.

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:07.440
I'll give the President a chance to talk about
all the good stuff that's happened, but I

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:10.070
just want you to know one thing.

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:14.170
In the last three years, just as we started
doing this, inflation in health care costs

00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:19.820
has dropped to 4 percent for three years in
a row for the first time in 50 years.

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:20.820
Fifty years.

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:21.820
(Applause.)

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:28.970
Before that, the costs were going up at three
times the rate of inflation for a decade.

00:12:28.970 --> 00:12:31.460
So now what?

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:34.090
What are you going to do on October 1st?

00:12:34.090 --> 00:12:36.000
Tell them how this has got to work.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:40.320
PRESIDENT OBAMA: Well, let me give folks just
a little bit of background about what's already

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:45.190
in place and then what happens on October
1st.

00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:52.220
When we passed the Affordable Care Act, there
were a number of components to it.

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:59.990
A big part of it was essentially providing
a Patient's Bill of Rights that Americans

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:03.810
and advocates have been fighting for for decades.

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:08.560
So what we wanted to do was make sure if you
already have health insurance that you get

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:13.050
a fair deal, that you're being treated well
by your insurers.

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:20.250
So we eliminated, prohibited insurance companies
from imposing lifetime limits, which oftentimes

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:24.870
if a family member really got sick, they thought
they were covered until suddenly they hit

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:29.900
that limit and now they're out hundreds of
thousands of dollars with no way of paying.

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:36.230
We said to insurance companies, you've got
to use at least 80 percent of your premium

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:42.800
that you're receiving on actual health care
-- not on administrative costs and CEO bonuses.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:50.220
And if you don't, you've got to rebate anything
that you spent back to the consumer.

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:53.200
So there are millions of Americans who've
received rebates.

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:59.230
They may not know that they got it because
of the Affordable Care Act -- or "Obamacare"

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:06.160
-- but they're pretty happy to get those rebates
back, because it made sure that the insurance

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:09.560
companies were treating folks fairly.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:17.030
We said that any young person who doesn't
have health insurance can stay on their parent's

00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:21.930
health insurance until they're 26 years old.

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:30.540
And as a consequence, what we've seen is steadily
the rate of uninsured for young people dropping

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:36.640
over the last three years since the bill passed
and obviously providing a lot of relief to

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:40.610
a lot of parents out there because a lot of
young people, as they've been entering into

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:47.220
the job market at a time when jobs are tough
to get and oftentimes benefits are slim, this

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:55.670
is providing enormous security until they
get more firmly established in the labor market.

00:14:55.670 --> 00:15:01.510
We provided additional discounts for prescription
drugs for seniors under the Medicare program.

00:15:01.510 --> 00:15:08.520
And so seniors have saved billions of dollars
when it comes to their prescription drugs.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:17.130
So there have been over the last three years
a whole array of consumer protections and

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:22.070
savings for consumers that result directly
from the law that we passed.

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:26.370
And for those who say that they want to repeal
it, typically when you ask them about, what

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:29.690
are all these various benefits, they say,
well, that one is good, and that one is pretty

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:31.650
good, and we'd keep that.

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:36.860
And you pretty much go down the list, and
there's not too much people object to.

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:43.840
You will recall also at the time that part
of the way that we paid for the health care

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:49.730
bill was we said Medicare is wasting a lot
of money without making seniors healthier.

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:54.680
And there was a lot of hue and cry about how
we were taking money out of Medicare -- well,

00:15:54.680 --> 00:16:05.220
it turns out that we were right, that we could
change how doctors and hospitals and providers

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:11.360
were operating, rewarding them for outcomes,
as opposed to simply how many procedures that

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:13.660
they did.

00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:20.700
You started seeing practices change among
millions of providers across the country.

00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:26.730
Medicare rates have actually slowed in terms
of inflation.

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:29.760
Seniors have saved money.

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:32.370
Folks are healthier.

00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:35.590
And some of those savings we've been able
to use to make sure that people who don't

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:37.530
have health insurance get health insurance.

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:41.090
Now, this brings me to October 1st.

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:51.230
The one part of the Affordable Care Act that
required several years to set up, but a critical

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:58.021
part, was how do we provide health insurance
for individuals who don't get health insurance

00:16:58.021 --> 00:16:59.021
through the job?

00:16:59.021 --> 00:17:06.350
It's a historical accident that in this country
health care is attached to employers.

00:17:06.350 --> 00:17:12.010
And part of the problem is if you're out there
shopping for health insurance on your own,

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:18.370
you're not part of a big pool, well, there's
no aggregation of risk taking place for the

00:17:18.370 --> 00:17:19.370
insurers.

00:17:19.370 --> 00:17:23.360
So they're basically going to say, let's see,
you're 50 years old.

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:26.190
You got high blood pressure.

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:29.810
And we just look at the actuarial tables and
we figure you're going to get sick, so we're

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:34.750
going to charge you $1,500 a month for health
insurance, which the average person has no

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:38.750
way of affording -- because there's no pooling
of risk.

00:17:38.750 --> 00:17:46.400
So what we said was we need to set up a mechanism
to pool people who currently don't have health

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:50.420
insurance so that they have the same purchasing
power, the same leverage that a big companies

00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:53.730
does when they're negotiating with the insurance
company.

00:17:53.730 --> 00:18:00.410
And essentially what we've done is we've created
what we're calling marketplaces in every state

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:08.250
across the country where consumers are now
able to be part of a big pool.

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:14.160
Insurers have to bid, essentially compete
for the business of that pool.

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:24.330
And what we now have set up are these marketplaces
that provide high-quality health care at affordable

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:29.059
prices, giving people choices so that they
can get the health insurance that they need

00:18:29.059 --> 00:18:30.410
and they want.

00:18:30.410 --> 00:18:36.780
And the premiums are significantly lower than
what they were able to previously get.

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:41.140
I'll take the example of New York State.

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:46.970
The insurers put in their bids to participate
in these marketplaces.

00:18:46.970 --> 00:18:54.720
It turns out that their rates are up to 50
percent lower than what was available previously

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:57.770
if you just went on the open market and you
tried to get health insurance.

00:18:57.770 --> 00:18:58.770
(Applause.)

00:18:58.770 --> 00:19:01.590
Fifty percent lower in this state.

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:04.650
California -- it's about 33 percent lower.

00:19:04.650 --> 00:19:09.440
In my home state of Illinois, they just announced
it's about 25 percent lower.

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:15.400
So just by pooling and creating competition
so that insurers have to go after people's

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:25.140
business the way they go after a group plan,
we have drastically reduced premiums and costs.

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:30.110
On top of that, what we're now doing is we're
saying if with the better deal that you got

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:35.250
you still can't afford it, we're going to
give you tax credits to essentially subsidize

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:38.930
your purchase of health insurance.

00:19:38.930 --> 00:19:40.330
And here's the net result.

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:47.460
We'll be continuing to roll out what the actual
prices are going to end up being, but I can

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:53.750
tell you right now that in many states across
the country, if you're, say, a 27-year-old

00:19:53.750 --> 00:20:00.900
young woman, don't have health insurance,
you get on that exchange, you're going to

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:06.920
be able to purchase high-quality health insurance
for less than the cost of your cellphone bill.

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:10.890
And because all the insurers who participate
are required to, for example, provide free

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:18.630
preventive care, free contraceptive care,
that young woman, she may make up what she's

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:23.260
spending on premiums just on her monthly use
of health care.

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:29.179
So this is going to be a good deal for those
who don't have health insurance.

00:20:29.179 --> 00:20:32.720
Those who already have health insurance get
better health insurance.

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:39.370
And the best part of the whole thing is, because
of these changes we initiated in terms of

00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:46.730
how we're paying providers, health care costs
have grown, as you pointed out, Mr. President,

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:50.429
at the slowest rate in 50 years.

00:20:50.429 --> 00:20:55.450
We are bending the cost curve and getting
at the problems that are creating our deficits

00:20:55.450 --> 00:20:58.800
in Medicare and Medicaid.

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:00.340
(Applause.)

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:07.060
PRESIDENT CLINTON: I should point out that,
so far, in most states, one of the good things

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:14.540
that at least I didn't know whether it would
happen is when we began this in the United

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:20.710
States, more than 80 percent of the American
states had only one or two companies providing

00:21:20.710 --> 00:21:24.860
health insurance who had more than 80 percent
of the market.

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:28.790
So there was, in effect, no price competition.

00:21:28.790 --> 00:21:33.730
So what I was terrified of was we'd open these
things and there would only be one company

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:38.270
show up and bid, and this whole thing, we'd
be having an academic conversation.

00:21:38.270 --> 00:21:43.230
Instead, it's actually led to the establishment
of more companies doing more bidding.

00:21:43.230 --> 00:21:47.720
And I think part of it is they have greater
confidence that they can deliver health care

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:50.230
at a more modest cost.

00:21:50.230 --> 00:21:54.150
So, so far, it's good.

00:21:54.150 --> 00:22:02.630
But I think it's important for you to the
tell the people why we're doing all this outreach

00:22:02.630 --> 00:22:08.900
-- because this only works, for example, if
young people show up.

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:13.030
And even if they buy their cheapest plan,
then they claim their tax credits, so it won't

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:15.240
cost them much -- 100 bucks a month or so.

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:21.760
We got to have them in the pools, because
otherwise all these projected low costs cannot

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:29.220
be held if older people with preexisting conditions
are disproportionately represented in any

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:30.220
given state.

00:22:30.220 --> 00:22:32.300
You've got to have everybody lined up.

00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:38.550
So explain what kind of -- all the work you've
been doing on the outreach for the opening

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:39.809
on October.

00:22:39.809 --> 00:22:45.710
PRESIDENT OBAMA: I think President Clinton
makes a really important point.

00:22:45.710 --> 00:23:00.059
And the way pools work, any pool, is essentially
those of us who are healthy subsidize somebody

00:23:00.059 --> 00:23:01.690
who is sick at any given time.

00:23:01.690 --> 00:23:06.290
We do that because we anticipate, well, at
some point we'll get sick and hope the healthy

00:23:06.290 --> 00:23:11.150
person is in our pool so those costs and those
risks get spread.

00:23:11.150 --> 00:23:14.250
That's what insurance is all about.

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:25.600
And what happens is if you don't have pools
that are a cross-section of society, then

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:30.300
people who are already sick or more likely
to get sick, they'll all rush out and buy

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:31.300
insurance.

00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:36.190
People who are healthy, they say, you know
what, I won't bother.

00:23:36.190 --> 00:23:39.270
And you get what's called adverse selection.

00:23:39.270 --> 00:23:43.860
Essentially what happens is that the premiums
start going higher and higher because the

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:46.929
risks aren't spread broadly enough across
the population.

00:23:46.929 --> 00:23:50.140
So you want to get a good cross-section in
every pool.

00:23:50.140 --> 00:23:54.250
That's why big companies have an easier time
getting good rates for their employees than

00:23:54.250 --> 00:23:59.130
small companies, because if you only have
five employees and one person is stricken

00:23:59.130 --> 00:24:03.230
with breast cancer, let's say, your rates
potentially shoot up.

00:24:03.230 --> 00:24:07.890
But if it's a thousand employees, then it
gets spread out.

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:12.370
So on October 1st, open enrollment begins.

00:24:12.370 --> 00:24:16.830
All these folks can start signing up for the
marketplace.

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:24.540
And what we want to make sure of is that everybody,
in every category, in every age group, understands

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:32.070
why health insurance is important, understands
why they should sign up, understands the choices

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:33.580
that are going to be available to them.

00:24:33.580 --> 00:24:41.860
They're going to be able to go to a computer,
tap on the web page and they're going to be

00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:48.190
able to shop just like you shopped for an
airline ticket or a flat-screen TV, and see

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:53.420
what's the best price for you, what's the
plan that's best suited for you, and go ahead

00:24:53.420 --> 00:24:55.929
and sign up right there and then.

00:24:55.929 --> 00:25:03.309
And that the open enrollment period will last
from October 1st until the end of March and

00:25:03.309 --> 00:25:07.570
so there will be six months for folks to sign
up.

00:25:07.570 --> 00:25:12.840
Normally, this would be pretty straightforward.

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:15.160
A lot of people don't have health insurance.

00:25:15.160 --> 00:25:19.710
A lot of people realize they should get health
insurance.

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:24.850
But let's face it, it's been a little political,
this whole Obamacare thing.

00:25:24.850 --> 00:25:25.850
(Laughter.)

00:25:25.850 --> 00:25:32.309
And so what you've had is an unprecedented
effort that you've seen ramp up over the last

00:25:32.309 --> 00:25:39.820
month or so in which those who have opposed
the idea of universal health care in the first

00:25:39.820 --> 00:25:44.020
place and have fought this thing tooth and
nail through Congress and through the courts

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:51.120
and so forth have been trying to scare and
discourage people from getting a good deal.

00:25:51.120 --> 00:25:58.000
And some of you may have seen some of the
commercials out there that are a little whacky.

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:04.420
And the main message we have -- and we're
using social media, we're talking to churches,

00:26:04.420 --> 00:26:11.720
we're talking to various civic groups -- and
what we're saying to people is, look, just

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:12.720
go to the website yourself.

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:20.549
Go to healthcare.gov; take a look at whether
this is a good deal or not and make your own

00:26:20.549 --> 00:26:23.370
decision about whether this is good for you.

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:29.090
Because what we are confident about is that
when people look and see that they can get

00:26:29.090 --> 00:26:35.500
high-quality, affordable health care for less
than their cell phone bill, they're going

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:36.500
to sign up.

00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:38.570
They are going to sign up.

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:43.860
And part of what I think the resistance that
we've seen ramp up particularly over the last

00:26:43.860 --> 00:26:47.510
couple of months is all about is the opponents
of health care reform know they're going to

00:26:47.510 --> 00:26:48.510
sign up.

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:52.610
In fact, one of the major opponents, when
asked, well, why is it that you'd potentially

00:26:52.610 --> 00:27:00.400
shut down the government at this point just
to block Obamacare, he basically fessed up.

00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:07.140
He said, well, once consumers get hooked on
having health insurance and subsidies, then

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:09.580
they won't want to give it up.

00:27:09.580 --> 00:27:10.580
(Laughter.)

00:27:10.580 --> 00:27:12.960
I mean, that's -- you can look at the transcript.

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:16.100
This is one of the major opponents of health
care reform.

00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:18.410
It is an odd logic.

00:27:18.410 --> 00:27:24.799
Essentially they're saying people will like
this thing too much and then it will be really

00:27:24.799 --> 00:27:26.830
hard to roll back.

00:27:26.830 --> 00:27:34.809
So it is very important that people just know
what's out there, what's available to them,

00:27:34.809 --> 00:27:38.640
and let people make up their own minds as
to whether it makes sense or not.

00:27:38.640 --> 00:27:43.830
Now, one last thing I want to say, because
I do think sometimes -- people come up to

00:27:43.830 --> 00:27:51.070
me and they say, well, if this is such a good
deal, how come the polls show that it's not

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:52.070
popular?

00:27:52.070 --> 00:27:57.850
Well, one of the things you and I both know
is that when you come to health care, there's

00:27:57.850 --> 00:28:00.320
no more personal and intimate decision for
people.

00:28:00.320 --> 00:28:03.100
I mean, this is something that people really
care about.

00:28:03.100 --> 00:28:09.240
And frankly, the devil you know is always
better than the devil you don't know.

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:14.440
And that's what "Harry and Louise" was all
about back in the '90s, right?

00:28:14.440 --> 00:28:19.700
It was scaring people with the prospect of
change.

00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:27.410
And so part of our goal here is just to make
sure people have good information.

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:34.130
And there has been billions of dollars spent
making people scared and worried about this

00:28:34.130 --> 00:28:36.000
stuff.

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:43.710
And rather than trying to disabuse people
of every single bit of misinformation that's

00:28:43.710 --> 00:28:46.490
been out there, what we're saying is just
look for yourself.

00:28:46.490 --> 00:28:52.130
Take a look at it and you will discover that
this is a good deal for you.

00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:56.340
PRESIDENT CLINTON: Well, first of all, I completely
agree with that.

00:28:56.340 --> 00:29:00.830
I think we've got to just drive people to
the websites.

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:05.929
The states that are participating -- the Supreme
Court decision that upheld the health care

00:29:05.929 --> 00:29:12.700
law said that states didn't have to set up
these marketplaces if they didn't want to,

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:15.690
but if they didn't the federal government
would set it up.

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:23.419
They also said that states didn't have to
expand Medicaid coverage to help people whose

00:29:23.419 --> 00:29:28.880
incomes are up to 138 percent of the federal
poverty level buy health insurance.

00:29:28.880 --> 00:29:35.429
There are some states, believe it or not,
that want the marketplace but don't want the

00:29:35.429 --> 00:29:36.890
Medicaid.

00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:40.830
And that's going to lead to a cruel result,
and there's nothing the President can do and

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:41.830
it's not his fault.

00:29:41.830 --> 00:29:42.830
That's what the Supreme Court said.

00:29:42.830 --> 00:29:48.780
So we can have this bizarre situation where,
let's say, a business with 60 employees can

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:56.710
-- or an individual going into the individual
market will get the benefit of tax credits

00:29:56.710 --> 00:30:02.280
for everybody with incomes of 138 percent
of the federal poverty level or above, but

00:30:02.280 --> 00:30:05.720
they won't get it for people who are between
100 and 138 percent.

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:10.980
So lower-income people who desperately need
the health insurance -- we would have the

00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:13.440
cruelest of all situations in those states.

00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:17.750
And there's nothing the President can do about
it because of the Supreme Court decision.

00:30:17.750 --> 00:30:20.050
So we have to persuade the states to come
on.

00:30:20.050 --> 00:30:24.809
But more and more states with Republican governors,
Republican legislatures are doing it.

00:30:24.809 --> 00:30:29.049
Tell them about Arkansas, because we're doing
well down there.

00:30:29.049 --> 00:30:30.049
(Laughter.)

00:30:30.049 --> 00:30:35.040
PRESIDENT OBAMA: A little hometown bias, there's
nothing wrong with that.

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:36.040
(Laughter.)

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:39.400
A couple of things that are happening that
I think are very interesting.

00:30:39.400 --> 00:30:45.150
First of all, look, I'm sympathetic to some
of these Republican governors who are under

00:30:45.150 --> 00:30:52.100
a lot of pressure because the whole issue
of whether you're for Obamacare or not has

00:30:52.100 --> 00:30:54.870
become a litmus test in the other party.

00:30:54.870 --> 00:31:01.460
So some of them, politically it's been tough;
sometimes state legislatures that refuse to

00:31:01.460 --> 00:31:06.830
allow governors to go ahead and implement.

00:31:06.830 --> 00:31:13.990
But as you indicated, what we've seen is that
when Republican governors take a look at the

00:31:13.990 --> 00:31:19.910
deal they're getting where, in addition to
these exchanges, we're also providing a much

00:31:19.910 --> 00:31:25.780
more significant match, much more federal
money to provide health insurance -- from

00:31:25.780 --> 00:31:29.600
the state's perspective, they're not paying;
the federal government is picking up the tab

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:35.580
-- and this is helping them because people
are no longer going to the emergency room

00:31:35.580 --> 00:31:38.320
and they now have good health care, they're
now getting preventive care.

00:31:38.320 --> 00:31:44.251
You're seeing some Republican governors step
up and saying, I may not like Obamacare, but

00:31:44.251 --> 00:31:52.500
I'm going to go ahead and make sure that my
people are benefiting from this plan.

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:53.870
So that's one good thing that's happening.

00:31:53.870 --> 00:31:58.810
The second thing that's happening is there
are a couple of states -- Arkansas is a good

00:31:58.810 --> 00:32:06.440
example; Kentucky is another good example;
Idaho, interesting example -- these are states

00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:09.220
where I just got beat.

00:32:09.220 --> 00:32:16.890
I mean, I do not have a big constituency in
these states.

00:32:16.890 --> 00:32:17.890
(Laughter.)

00:32:17.890 --> 00:32:18.890
Well, I take that back.

00:32:18.890 --> 00:32:23.429
You know what, 40 percent is still a lot of
people -- but I'm losing by 20 percent in

00:32:23.429 --> 00:32:27.570
these states.

00:32:27.570 --> 00:32:34.000
But the governors were still able to say we're
going to set up our own state exchanges, their

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:36.820
own marketplaces.

00:32:36.820 --> 00:32:42.429
And each state is just using their own name
for it.

00:32:42.429 --> 00:32:49.429
So I had a meet -- I had a conference, a video
conference with all the state directors of

00:32:49.429 --> 00:32:55.510
all the marketplaces, and I'm talking to the
director in Kentucky and Idaho.

00:32:55.510 --> 00:32:58.570
And in Kentucky, it's called like, Kentucky
Connect.

00:32:58.570 --> 00:33:02.440
And in Idaho, it's called the Idaho Health
Care Exchange.

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:08.840
And there's a story that came out of Kentucky
where some folks were signing people up at

00:33:08.840 --> 00:33:11.030
a county fair somewhere.

00:33:11.030 --> 00:33:16.040
Some guy goes up and he starts looking at
the rates and decides he's going to sign up.

00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:18.770
And he turns to his friend and said, this
is a great deal.

00:33:18.770 --> 00:33:21.950
This is a lot better than Obamacare.

00:33:21.950 --> 00:33:23.240
(Laughter.)

00:33:23.240 --> 00:33:24.540
Right?

00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:26.240
Which is fine.

00:33:26.240 --> 00:33:27.549
(Laughter.)

00:33:27.549 --> 00:33:32.429
Because we -- I don't have pride of authorship
on this thing.

00:33:32.429 --> 00:33:34.660
I just want the thing to work.

00:33:34.660 --> 00:33:42.250
And Arkansas just came out with its rates,
and as has been true in virtually every single

00:33:42.250 --> 00:33:49.320
state, not only are premiums lower than they
were, they're a lot lower than even the most

00:33:49.320 --> 00:33:53.810
optimistic predictions were about how low
they would be.

00:33:53.810 --> 00:34:03.640
And once these marketplaces are up and running,
it turns out that what has traditionally been

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:10.760
a pretty conservative principle, which is
competition and choice work, well, in the

00:34:10.760 --> 00:34:13.730
insurance market, competition and choice work.

00:34:13.730 --> 00:34:20.139
And what we're seeing is that people are going
to be able to get the kind of health care

00:34:20.139 --> 00:34:22.560
that they have never been able to get before.

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:28.409
States are going to benefit from it because
they're going to save money.

00:34:28.409 --> 00:34:32.099
And one thing that all of you -- there are
probably very few people in this room who

00:34:32.099 --> 00:34:36.520
don't have health insurance, although if you
don't, you should sign up starting on October

00:34:36.520 --> 00:34:37.659
1st.

00:34:37.659 --> 00:34:38.809
(Laughter.)

00:34:38.809 --> 00:34:51.270
One of the things that many people don't realize
is that the subsidy that all of you provide

00:34:51.270 --> 00:34:57.720
for the uninsured is about $1,000 per family.

00:34:57.720 --> 00:35:04.340
You pay $1,000 -- everybody here who has got
health insurance pays about $1,000 more for

00:35:04.340 --> 00:35:12.109
your families' insurance than you otherwise
would have, because hospitals are mandated,

00:35:12.109 --> 00:35:17.100
they are required to provide service to anybody
who shows up.

00:35:17.100 --> 00:35:20.980
And so what happens is when you've got 15
percent of the population without health insurance,

00:35:20.980 --> 00:35:24.940
they end up showing up at the emergency room
typically at a point when they're much sicker

00:35:24.940 --> 00:35:29.030
than if they had been getting regular checkups
and preventive care.

00:35:29.030 --> 00:35:34.010
So you pay for the most expensive care there
is, because hospitals have got to recoup that

00:35:34.010 --> 00:35:35.730
money somewhere.

00:35:35.730 --> 00:35:41.880
And the way they do it is to charge higher
prices.

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:45.030
And people who have health insurance end up
picking it up.

00:35:45.030 --> 00:35:51.619
So part of what will help reduce the increase
in health care costs is making sure that that

00:35:51.619 --> 00:35:54.060
hidden subsidy no longer exists.

00:35:54.060 --> 00:35:59.300
PRESIDENT CLINTON: Let's talk a little bit
about business, because we're out of time,

00:35:59.300 --> 00:36:01.849
but I think it's really important.

00:36:01.849 --> 00:36:07.510
As you pointed out, most people who have insurance
work for a living, or somebody in their family

00:36:07.510 --> 00:36:11.680
does, and they get their insurance through
their workplace.

00:36:11.680 --> 00:36:19.859
The law says that all employers have to participate
if they have 50 employees or more.

00:36:19.859 --> 00:36:26.890
Many employers with fewer than 50 employees
already voluntarily provide some health insurance.

00:36:26.890 --> 00:36:32.950
Both the companies with 50 or more and the
companies with fewer than 50 are somewhat

00:36:32.950 --> 00:36:33.950
concerned.

00:36:33.950 --> 00:36:39.670
And the employees that have to be insured
are those who work 30 hours a week or more.

00:36:39.670 --> 00:36:46.441
So there were many people who speculated that
when this law came into place that it would

00:36:46.441 --> 00:36:52.210
add to the cost and there would be a lot more
part-time workers instead of full-time workers.

00:36:52.210 --> 00:36:58.210
I'll save the President the time in the interview
on this -- so far, that's not true.

00:36:58.210 --> 00:37:02.720
The overwhelming number of people who have
been hired coming out of this recession have

00:37:02.720 --> 00:37:08.050
been -- they have been hired at lower wages,
but they have been full-time employees.

00:37:08.050 --> 00:37:14.710
There has not been an increase in the percentage
of our employment in part-time work.

00:37:14.710 --> 00:37:20.410
There has been an increase in relatively lower-wage
new jobs.

00:37:20.410 --> 00:37:23.200
But that means they need health insurance
even more.

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:31.070
So explain very briefly to them how this is
going to work, how private employers are helped

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:33.270
to buy their insurance and the requirements.

00:37:33.270 --> 00:37:38.680
PRESIDENT OBAMA: Well, first of all, if you're
a large employer or an employer with more

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:45.390
than 50 employees, you're already providing
health insurance, you don't have to do anything

00:37:45.390 --> 00:37:52.780
other than just make sure that you can show
that you're providing health insurance.

00:37:52.780 --> 00:37:58.369
And there was a lot of news recently about
how we delayed the so-called employer mandate

00:37:58.369 --> 00:38:04.310
for a year -- because under the law, what
it says is if you have more than 50 employees,

00:38:04.310 --> 00:38:08.740
you're not providing health insurance to your
employees, then you're going to pay a penalty

00:38:08.740 --> 00:38:18.150
to help pay for the fact that we, the taxpayers,
are going to have to provide your employees

00:38:18.150 --> 00:38:23.970
with health insurance -- which, by the way,
is only fair.

00:38:23.970 --> 00:38:30.290
A lot of the controversy around the Affordable
Care Act had to do with these so-called mandates,

00:38:30.290 --> 00:38:33.460
both an employer mandate and an individual
mandate.

00:38:33.460 --> 00:38:43.109
And the employer mandate says, if you don't
meet your responsibilities by your employees,

00:38:43.109 --> 00:38:48.190
and they end up getting Medicaid or they're
ending up in the emergency room, you're basically

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:50.770
dumping those costs onto society.

00:38:50.770 --> 00:38:51.770
That's not fair.

00:38:51.770 --> 00:38:56.420
So we're going to charge you a couple thousand
dollars to help pay for health care for those

00:38:56.420 --> 00:38:57.490
employees.

00:38:57.490 --> 00:39:05.190
To the individuals, what we said was we're
going to make health insurance so affordable,

00:39:05.190 --> 00:39:10.960
so cheap for you, so heavily subsidized if
you're not making a lot of money, that if

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:17.000
you're not getting health insurance then it's
because you just decided you don't want to,

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:19.880
you don't need to.

00:39:19.880 --> 00:39:29.200
And in that circumstance, what happens when
you get hit by a bus, heaven forbid, or somebody

00:39:29.200 --> 00:39:32.090
in your family gets sick, and you hadn't had
them covered?

00:39:32.090 --> 00:39:35.100
Well, we're going to end up having to pay
for you anyway because we're not going to

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:40.760
just let somebody bleed in front of the emergency
room.

00:39:40.760 --> 00:39:47.210
So what we've said is you've got to take responsibility,
and so there's a small penalty if you don't

00:39:47.210 --> 00:39:48.570
get health insurance.

00:39:48.570 --> 00:39:53.170
This is where a lot of the controversy and
unpopularity came in, because people generally

00:39:53.170 --> 00:39:57.990
don't like to be told, "you've got to get
health insurance," and employers don't like

00:39:57.990 --> 00:40:01.330
be told, "you've got to give your employees
health insurance."

00:40:01.330 --> 00:40:10.750
But, as a society, what we cannot do is to
say, you have no responsibilities whatsoever,

00:40:10.750 --> 00:40:14.950
but you've got guaranteed coverage.

00:40:14.950 --> 00:40:18.900
And this raises the whole issue of preexisting
conditions, which we haven't talked a lot

00:40:18.900 --> 00:40:24.109
about but is really important.

00:40:24.109 --> 00:40:32.151
One of the central components of this law,
one of the main perversities of the health

00:40:32.151 --> 00:40:38.560
care system before this law passed was there
were millions of people around the country

00:40:38.560 --> 00:40:46.810
who, if you had gotten sick before, if you
had had a heart attack, if you had had cancer,

00:40:46.810 --> 00:40:55.980
if you had diabetes; let's say, when it first
happened you had a job, you got cured; then

00:40:55.980 --> 00:40:59.390
you lose your job or you're trying to change
jobs or you're trying to start a business,

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:03.040
you try to go out and get health insurance,
the health insurance company not only could

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:05.820
deny you but had every incentive to deny you.

00:41:05.820 --> 00:41:13.340
Because, basically, they'd rather have healthy
people who are paying premiums and never asking

00:41:13.340 --> 00:41:14.640
for a payout.

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:20.640
They don't want somebody who actuarially they
can anticipate might get sick.

00:41:20.640 --> 00:41:25.869
And so, keep in mind that a huge percentage
of our society has some sort of preexisting

00:41:25.869 --> 00:41:32.210
condition, and they can be locked out.

00:41:32.210 --> 00:41:37.270
You can do everything right, work hard, build
a strong middle-class life, but if you've

00:41:37.270 --> 00:41:42.619
been sick and then you lose your job or something
happens, you may suddenly be locked out of

00:41:42.619 --> 00:41:49.170
the insurance market, or the premiums may
be so high that only somebody fabulously rich

00:41:49.170 --> 00:41:50.770
could afford it.

00:41:50.770 --> 00:41:57.570
So what we said is, all right, you know what,
insurance companies, you can no longer bar

00:41:57.570 --> 00:42:02.740
somebody from getting health insurance just
because they've got a preexisting condition.

00:42:02.740 --> 00:42:11.130
But the only way that works is if everybody
had a requirement to get health insurance.

00:42:11.130 --> 00:42:14.230
Because think about what happens if you don't
have that rule.

00:42:14.230 --> 00:42:19.860
Well, all of us -- not all of us, but a lot
of us who were trying to figure out how to

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:24.330
save some money would say, well, I'm not going
to worry about it until I get sick, and then

00:42:24.330 --> 00:42:29.500
right when I'm diagnosed with something that's
going to be expensive I'll go to the insurance

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:33.750
company and say, you can't prevent me from
getting health insurance just because I've

00:42:33.750 --> 00:42:35.400
got a preexisting condition.

00:42:35.400 --> 00:42:41.540
So they could potentially game the system
and it wouldn't work.

00:42:41.540 --> 00:42:47.241
So now what we've done is said you've got
to provide health insurance to anybody, all

00:42:47.241 --> 00:42:49.550
comers -- that's the deal.

00:42:49.550 --> 00:42:53.650
The flipside of it is everybody has got some
responsibility and we'll help you pay for

00:42:53.650 --> 00:42:55.510
it to get health insurance.

00:42:55.510 --> 00:43:02.900
And that's where a lot of the misunderstandings,
the frustrations about health care reform

00:43:02.900 --> 00:43:03.900
came in.

00:43:03.900 --> 00:43:11.400
I should add, by the way, that this was the
same proposition that was set up in Massachusetts

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:15.460
under a governor named Mitt Romney that's
working really well.

00:43:15.460 --> 00:43:18.140
Ninety-nine percent of the people in Massachusetts
have coverage.

00:43:18.140 --> 00:43:28.380
And that same principle was, ironically, considered
a very smart Republican conservative principle.

00:43:28.380 --> 00:43:29.430
But it was the right one.

00:43:29.430 --> 00:43:31.170
The economics of it are true.

00:43:31.170 --> 00:43:36.480
So, just to finish up the question, when it
comes to businesses, if you're already providing

00:43:36.480 --> 00:43:39.980
health insurance for your employees, that's
great.

00:43:39.980 --> 00:43:44.520
You don't have do much other than just make
sure that you show us that you've got health

00:43:44.520 --> 00:43:46.020
insurance for your employees.

00:43:46.020 --> 00:43:50.830
If you have more than 50 employees, and you're
not providing health insurance for them, you

00:43:50.830 --> 00:43:55.911
now have the opportunity to join a pool of
small businesses to get a better price and

00:43:55.911 --> 00:43:58.550
a better deal on health insurance.

00:43:58.550 --> 00:44:03.300
You're eligible for tax credits in providing
health insurance to your employees.

00:44:03.300 --> 00:44:12.120
Up to 35 percent of the premiums for each
employee will be a tax benefit -- a tax credit

00:44:12.120 --> 00:44:14.660
from the federal government.

00:44:14.660 --> 00:44:18.150
But if you still aren't providing health insurance
for your employees after that, then we're

00:44:18.150 --> 00:44:22.470
going to go ahead and penalize you for it.

00:44:22.470 --> 00:44:26.119
And I can understand why some businesses wouldn't
want to pay for it.

00:44:26.119 --> 00:44:29.360
If they're not currently providing health
insurance for their employees, what that means

00:44:29.360 --> 00:44:35.859
is that they'd rather have those additional
profits than make sure that their employees

00:44:35.859 --> 00:44:39.480
are getting a fair deal.

00:44:39.480 --> 00:44:42.580
In some cases, they may be operating under
some very small margins.

00:44:42.580 --> 00:44:48.470
But keep in mind, since people are -- companies
are exempted, the average small business with

00:44:48.470 --> 00:44:53.420
five employees, mom-and-pop shop, 10 employees,
they're not under that requirement.

00:44:53.420 --> 00:44:59.720
So I'm not that sympathetic to a company,
typically, if it's got more than 50 employees

00:44:59.720 --> 00:45:04.160
and generating some significant revenue, we're
making it affordable for them to provide health

00:45:04.160 --> 00:45:05.220
insurance for their employees.

00:45:05.220 --> 00:45:06.720
They should do the right thing.

00:45:06.720 --> 00:45:07.720
(Applause.)

00:45:07.720 --> 00:45:13.280
PRESIDENT CLINTON: I agree with that.

00:45:13.280 --> 00:45:18.080
We have to close, but I think there's one
last issue we ought to deal with.

00:45:18.080 --> 00:45:22.970
The most important thing obviously is just
to get people enrolled in this.

00:45:22.970 --> 00:45:24.650
We'll work through it as we go along.

00:45:24.650 --> 00:45:34.410
But you just heard the President say that
so far in virtually every state, the actual

00:45:34.410 --> 00:45:40.600
prices of the insurance are coming in quite
a bit lower than they were originally estimated

00:45:40.600 --> 00:45:43.060
to.

00:45:43.060 --> 00:45:48.829
With the original price estimates and with
the government obligated to provide subsidies

00:45:48.829 --> 00:45:52.930
-- which costs money on the budget, right?

00:45:52.930 --> 00:46:00.390
-- it was, nonetheless, estimated that in
the first 10 years, this would keep the national

00:46:00.390 --> 00:46:06.330
debt $110 billion lower than it otherwise
would have been, which means if we come in

00:46:06.330 --> 00:46:12.690
at even less, we can bring the debt down more,
or we can subsidize more small businesses

00:46:12.690 --> 00:46:17.950
and get more small businesses into this loop.

00:46:17.950 --> 00:46:27.119
A lot of people come up to me and say, now,
you sound like the people you used to criticize

00:46:27.119 --> 00:46:30.980
who say we could cut taxes all day long, increase
spending and balance the budget.

00:46:30.980 --> 00:46:33.420
Don't give me that; this sounds too good to
be true.

00:46:33.420 --> 00:46:41.540
So I think before you leave, you should tell
people how we can spend more -- not so much

00:46:41.540 --> 00:46:48.150
in direct spending, but in tax credits -- and
still wind up reducing overall federal spending

00:46:48.150 --> 00:46:50.430
by $110 billion during this decade.

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:53.550
PRESIDENT OBAMA: Well, a couple of things
just in terms of how this whole thing got

00:46:53.550 --> 00:46:54.550
paid for.

00:46:54.550 --> 00:47:02.750
First of all, I think it's really important
to point out here that the total cost of the

00:47:02.750 --> 00:47:09.261
Affordable Care Act to provide health insurance
for every American out there at an affordable

00:47:09.261 --> 00:47:18.280
rate is costing about the same amount over
the course of 10 years as the cost of the

00:47:18.280 --> 00:47:26.890
prescription drug bill that President Bush
passed -- except that wasn't paid for.

00:47:26.890 --> 00:47:30.920
We felt obliged to actually pay for it and
not just add to the deficit.

00:47:30.920 --> 00:47:31.920
(Applause.)

00:47:31.920 --> 00:47:37.400
So what we did -- it's paid for by a combination
of things.

00:47:37.400 --> 00:47:40.720
We did raise taxes on some things.

00:47:40.720 --> 00:47:50.900
We, for example, said that for high-end income
individuals, you can pay a slightly higher

00:47:50.900 --> 00:47:53.780
Medicare rate -- Medicare tax.

00:47:53.780 --> 00:47:56.490
So we bumped that up a little bit.

00:47:56.490 --> 00:48:05.680
We said that for employers who are currently
providing a so-called Cadillac health care

00:48:05.680 --> 00:48:14.130
plan, where there are so many bells and whistles,
there's no incentive to actually spend wisely

00:48:14.130 --> 00:48:18.820
when it comes to health care -- we're actually
going to penalize you for that -- not only

00:48:18.820 --> 00:48:24.730
to raise a little bit of money, but also to
say you're encouraging the worst aspects of

00:48:24.730 --> 00:48:30.220
a health care system where you spend a lot
of money, you don't get better outcomes.

00:48:30.220 --> 00:48:33.490
I mentioned to you Medicare.

00:48:33.490 --> 00:48:40.600
We basically said -- there's a program in
Medicare called Medicare Advantage that provides

00:48:40.600 --> 00:48:46.859
some additional options for Medicare recipients
above and beyond standard Medicare.

00:48:46.859 --> 00:48:49.340
And it's very popular with a lot of seniors.

00:48:49.340 --> 00:48:54.470
You get eyeglasses and other benefits.

00:48:54.470 --> 00:49:00.619
But it turned out that it was so uncompetitive
that we were providing tens of billions of

00:49:00.619 --> 00:49:06.200
dollars of subsidies to the insurance companies
under this Medicare Advantage Plan without

00:49:06.200 --> 00:49:08.380
getting better outcomes, health outcomes for
seniors.

00:49:08.380 --> 00:49:12.980
So what we said was we'll keep Medicare Advantage
and we'll give them a small premium if they're

00:49:12.980 --> 00:49:17.040
providing better services for seniors, but
we're going to make you compete for it a little

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:18.040
bit.

00:49:18.040 --> 00:49:22.250
And we're going to save tens of billions of
dollars in the process, and that will go into

00:49:22.250 --> 00:49:23.810
paying for the Affordable Care Act.

00:49:23.810 --> 00:49:32.000
So the bottom line is, through these various
mechanisms we raised enough money to pay for

00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:37.610
providing health insurance for those who don't
have it, to provide these tax credits in the

00:49:37.610 --> 00:49:46.440
marketplace, and at the same time, because
we're driving down costs, we actually end

00:49:46.440 --> 00:49:47.440
up saving a little money.

00:49:47.440 --> 00:49:51.380
It is a net reduction of our deficit.

00:49:51.380 --> 00:49:57.300
The irony of those who are talking about repealing
Obamacare because it's so wildly expensive

00:49:57.300 --> 00:50:02.970
is if they actually repealed the law, it would
add to the deficit.

00:50:02.970 --> 00:50:04.960
It would add to the deficit.

00:50:04.960 --> 00:50:09.660
Now, there have been a couple of Republicans
in the House who have been smart enough to

00:50:09.660 --> 00:50:16.579
say, we're going to repeal all the benefits
so that 25, 30 million people don't get health

00:50:16.579 --> 00:50:21.020
insurance, but we're going to keep the taxes
that Obama raised, we just won't talk about

00:50:21.020 --> 00:50:22.150
that.

00:50:22.150 --> 00:50:26.310
And then, that way we can say we reduced the
deficit.

00:50:26.310 --> 00:50:33.180
But obviously, you're doing some funny business
there with the budget.

00:50:33.180 --> 00:50:36.590
But, look, nothing is free.

00:50:36.590 --> 00:50:41.960
The bottom line, though, is do we want to
continue to live in a society where we've

00:50:41.960 --> 00:50:48.410
got the most inefficient health care system
on Earth, leaving millions of people exposed

00:50:48.410 --> 00:50:52.480
to the possibilities that they could lose
everything because they get sick?

00:50:52.480 --> 00:50:58.359
Or we've got little children and families
going to the emergency room once a week because

00:50:58.359 --> 00:51:03.810
they've got asthma and other preventable diseases,
because their families aren't linked up with

00:51:03.810 --> 00:51:07.560
a primary care physician who is providing
them regular care?

00:51:07.560 --> 00:51:17.349
Where the costs to society for reduced productivity,
illnesses, et cetera, all burden our businesses?

00:51:17.349 --> 00:51:20.339
Is that the kind of society we aspire to?

00:51:20.339 --> 00:51:23.560
And I think the answer is no.

00:51:23.560 --> 00:51:33.359
And the notion that we would resist, or at
least some would resist as fiercely as they

00:51:33.359 --> 00:51:43.960
have, make this their number-one agenda, perpetuating
a system in which millions of people across

00:51:43.960 --> 00:51:52.099
the country, hardworking Americans don't have
access to health care I think is wrong.

00:51:52.099 --> 00:51:53.310
(Applause.)

00:51:53.310 --> 00:51:59.880
PRESIDENT CLINTON: We have to close.

00:51:59.880 --> 00:52:01.510
But I will close with a story.

00:52:01.510 --> 00:52:10.221
I told you all this morning that the employee
that our health access program lost in the

00:52:10.221 --> 00:52:13.040
Kenya mall shooting was a Dutch nurse.

00:52:13.040 --> 00:52:20.490
And we spend a lot of time in the Netherlands;
we get a lot of support there.

00:52:20.490 --> 00:52:23.680
Oxi is one of the biggest insurance companies
in Europe.

00:52:23.680 --> 00:52:26.120
They're one of our partners here.

00:52:26.120 --> 00:52:29.220
I went to celebrate their 200th anniversary
with them.

00:52:29.220 --> 00:52:35.400
They started as a fire insurance company with
39 farmers, 200 years ago.

00:52:35.400 --> 00:52:46.070
And we were out there in this big farm field
in a tent in the shadow of a 13th century

00:52:46.070 --> 00:52:48.970
church and a big Dutch windmill.

00:52:48.970 --> 00:52:53.940
And I asked the chairman of the company, I
said, do you write health insurance?

00:52:53.940 --> 00:52:58.230
Because in the Netherlands there's no Medicare
and no Medicaid, everybody is on an individual

00:52:58.230 --> 00:53:01.860
mandate and you just subsidize people based
on their incomes.

00:53:01.860 --> 00:53:04.140
He said, yes, I write it; we all do.

00:53:04.140 --> 00:53:07.790
And he looked at me and he said, but we don't
make any money on it.

00:53:07.790 --> 00:53:10.480
And he said, we shouldn't.

00:53:10.480 --> 00:53:15.270
This guy is running a huge -- can you imagine
somebody saying that in America?

00:53:15.270 --> 00:53:16.270
(Laughter.)

00:53:16.270 --> 00:53:17.310
He said, we shouldn't.

00:53:17.310 --> 00:53:20.980
If I can't make money on this business doing
traditional insurance business, I've got no

00:53:20.980 --> 00:53:21.980
business in the work.

00:53:21.980 --> 00:53:26.490
He said, look, health care is a public good
and you've got to find a way to finance it

00:53:26.490 --> 00:53:28.310
for everybody.

00:53:28.310 --> 00:53:29.310
(Applause.)

00:53:29.310 --> 00:53:35.340
And he said, it's just an intermediary function
that somebody has to handle.

00:53:35.340 --> 00:53:40.070
But in the end, it's how it's delivered, how
it's priced, and how healthy you can keep

00:53:40.070 --> 00:53:41.690
your people.

00:53:41.690 --> 00:53:47.870
So the First Lady is trying to keep us all
healthier, and you're trying to change the

00:53:47.870 --> 00:53:49.500
delivery and the pricing.

00:53:49.500 --> 00:53:52.430
And you have to cover everybody to do it.

00:53:52.430 --> 00:53:54.859
I think this is a big step forward for America.

00:53:54.859 --> 00:54:00.570
This will, over the next decade, not only
make us healthier, but it will free up in

00:54:00.570 --> 00:54:06.330
the private sector largely funds that can
then be reinvested in other areas of economic

00:54:06.330 --> 00:54:09.650
growth, and give us a much more well-balanced
economy.

00:54:09.650 --> 00:54:13.400
But, first, we've got to get everybody to
sign up.

00:54:13.400 --> 00:54:14.730
PRESIDENT OBAMA: Everybody, sign up.

00:54:14.730 --> 00:54:15.730
Go to healthcare.gov.

00:54:15.730 --> 00:54:16.900
Thank you very much.

00:54:16.900 --> 00:54:17.200
(Applause.)

